the overlays blending one land class into the next are left out;

water and every raster stay as they are, byte for byte;

the roads, forests, buildings and other objects are left out (with their definitions and the 32-bit pools of the
            roads' points): they go to the tile's overlay (), or come from another overlay package.

            Elevations X-Plane samples from the tile's raster stay sampled, so the relief is X-Plane's own.
            With , the textures near airports are of finer zooms (see ).

            Turns one X-Plane Global Scenery tile into an overlay DSF (sim/overlay 1): its roads, railways, power lines,
            forests, autogen and objects, without its terrain and rasters, for the overlay package beside the photo package.


every command but the terrain patches is copied byte for byte, pool and definition selections included;

the object, polygon and network tables and the 32-bit pools (the roads' points) stay as they are;

the 16-bit pools only the terrain used become one-point placeholders, so the others keep their indices;

the terrain and raster tables are emptied and the rasters left out.

            OrthoStreamXP reads the roads of an overlay package's plain DSFs to light the photo tiles at night, so the overlay is
            never packed.
